## Why does this matter?

For AI and other technology to be useful to people, businesses, and organizations in Iceland, it has to work in our language and be “literate” in the language we use in our communication, data, and workflows.\
Strong Icelandic capability is therefore a prerequisite for companies and organizations in Iceland to create value with the latest technology.\
Icelandic in technology is also an important accessibility issue, for example, for people with disabilities and for those who do not speak Icelandic as their first language or are learning it.

Under an agreement with the Ministry of Culture, Innovation and Higher Education, Almannarómur serves as Iceland's center for language technology, and has managed two Language Technology Programmes. These programs have produced open technical infrastructure that anyone developing technology in Icelandic can use.

## Core projects

“Core projects” is the working name for individual projects funded through the Language Technology Programme. Their goal is always to strengthen how well Icelandic is supported in technology, and their outputs must be released under open licenses. Core project outputs are published on CLARIN and on our GitHub and Hugging Face pages.\
The Language Technology Programme's steering committee sets the priorities for allocating funding to core projects. Its priorities for 2024–2026 are:

1. **Data collection: Continued development of Icelandic datasets, with more effort put into expanding the Icelandic Gigaword Corpus (Risamálheild) and adding sub-corpora.**
2. **Test data: More and better benchmarks for measuring how well different language technology solutions handle Icelandic.**
3. **Language technology for accessibility: A focus on developing solutions for people with disabilities and others who rely on technology in daily life.**

Do you have an idea or proposal for a core project? Get in touch → [almannaromur@almannaromur.is](mailto:almannaromur@almannaromur.is)

## Active projects

{% cards %}
{% flipcard title="Collecting speech data for Icelandic" note="Partners: Árni Magnússon Institute and Grammatek" %}
This project collects speech data along with matching text data. The data will be added to the Árni Magnússon Institute's Icelandic Gigaword Corpus, where it will be available for development and research.
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Real-time captioning" note="Partner: Miðeind" %}
This project is developing an open model that can take Icelandic speech and produce English subtitles in real time.
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Benchmarks for Icelandic" note="Partner: Árni Magnússon Institute" %}
This project is creating new benchmarks to measure how well language models handle Icelandic. Results will be published on the Language Technology Programme's shared dashboard, with the aim of giving the public and businesses better information about how Icelandic performs in different language technology solutions.
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Speech recognition for code-switching and proper names" note="Partners: Árni Magnússon Institute, Reykjavík University, and Tiro" %}
This project analyzes speech recognition errors caused by English words mixed into Icelandic (code-switching) and by proper names, and develops better training methods to solve the problem.
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Dataset and transcription for Icelandic Sign Language" note="Partners: University of Iceland, Reykjavík University, and the Communication Center for the Deaf and Hard of Hearing" %}
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Málvöllur" note="Partner: Málvöllur" %}
Development of an open platform where users can compare language model answers and vote on their quality. Results will be published on the Language Technology Programme's dashboard and will help users choose models.
{% /flipcard %}
{% flipcard title="Children's voices" note="Partner: Grammatek" %}
This project is developing two Icelandic children's voices, a boy's and a girl's, so that children who use communication devices can speak with voices that suit their age. The project is exempt from the open-license rule: for privacy reasons, the voices will only be available through professionals, to those who need them for health reasons.
{% /flipcard %}
{% /cards %}

## Completed projects

Four projects have already been completed during the 2024–2026 Language Technology Programme.

1. Test data and benchmarks for an open dashboard
2. Data collection for the Icelandic Gigaword Corpus
3. Data collection for recognizing natural, spontaneous speech
4. Data collection for children's voices in speech synthesis

